EXT. STAGE STATION WELL - HIGH DESERT - NOON

A parched yard. A plank shed, a rusted pump, a stone well with a wooden lid. Heat shimmers. A water barrel is tipped on its side, bone dry.

JEDEDIAH PRUITT (60s, hat brim curled, a sheriff's star pinned crooked) stands at the well with a rope around his waist. He peers over the edge. ABIGAIL "ABBY" TORRANCE (20s, dust-grey duster, rifle slung across her back) sits on the stone rim and drinks from a canteen. MR. LOCKHART (40s, striped vest, no gun) leans against the shed, watching the horizon.

JEDEDIAH
Pump's dry.

ABBY
Well's not.

JEDEDIAH
Well's got something in it.

ABBY
Something or someone?

Jedediah tests the rope. It holds. Lockhart pushes off the shed, steps forward, and bumps the barrel with his boot. It rolls across the yard and clunks against the pump.

LOCKHART
I'm only passing through, Sheriff. I'd consider it a favor if you'd leave my business out of it.

JEDEDIAH
Your business has been dropping bodies in my well for three weeks, Mr. Lockhart.

LOCKHART
I wouldn't know about that.

ABBY
(dry)
He wouldn't.

Jedediah lowers himself over the rim, hand over hand. Abby grips the rope and feeds it down. Lockhart takes a step toward her. She half-draws the rifle without looking at him, and he stops, both hands open at his sides.

LOCKHART
Miss, you really shouldn't.

ABBY
I really should.

From below, a splash. Then Jedediah's voice, hollow in the stone.

JEDEDIAH (O.S.)
It's him. It's Arliss.

Abby releases a breath. Lockhart spits in the dust. The wind picks up, rattling the shed door.

JEDEDIAH (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Pull me up.

Abby and Lockhart both look at the rope. Abby tightens her grip. Lockhart folds his arms. Neither of them moves.

ABBY
Mr. Lockhart, I'd hate to need your help.

LOCKHART
I'd hate to give it.

He takes hold of the rope anyway. They haul together, heaving, boots skidding in the dirt, until Jedediah's hat rises over the rim.
